tandem (4.50) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The .deb/.rpm inversion: each handler now knows WHOSE format the file is,
    which depends on the machine, not on Tandem. On Fedora/RHEL (dnf) and
    openSUSE (zypper) an .rpm is the system's OWN format, so `tandem-rpm` now
    INSTALLS it - ask, let the native manager pull the dependencies, confirm
    against rpm's own database - the mirror of how `tandem-deb` installs a .deb
    on apt. On the Debian family and on Arch an .rpm stays foreign, with the old
    explain-and-point-to-the-equivalent behaviour unchanged.
  * A .deb is foreign anywhere there is no apt. `tandem-deb` used to reach for
    apt regardless and fail later with "apt-get: command not found"; now, on a
    non-apt family, it says so plainly first - it is a Debian/Ubuntu package,
    your system uses dnf/pacman/zypper, look for the same program in your own
    manager or as a Flatpak.
  * Both fixes are robust where it counts: dnf5-based Fedora ships NO python3,
    so the python readers cannot run there. The .deb foreign guard is placed
    ABOVE the reader (a .deb is foreign whatever its contents), and the .rpm
    native path reads the package name with `rpm` itself (`t_rpm_nome_local`,
    no python). Two facts the real-distro test caught: dnf5 REJECTS the `--`
    separator (so the install command drops it; the path is absolute and safe),
    and Arch's `wine` is in `extra`, not multilib (so 4.49's guidance was right
    and needed no "fix"). Eight new messages, translated in all seven languages.
    Exercised end to end on real Fedora 41 and Arch containers: `tandem-rpm`
    installs a real .rpm through dnf with no python3, and `tandem-deb` explains
    a .deb as foreign on both.

