tandem (4.48) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The second step toward running on more than the Debian family: a way to
    install Tandem where there is no apt. Tandem is itself a `.deb`, so a
    Fedora, Arch or openSUSE user could not install it at all - 4.47 taught it
    to speak to them, but they still had no way to get it. build.py now also
    emits a distribution-agnostic bundle, `tandem_<version>_generic.tar.gz`:
    extract it and run `sudo ./install.sh`, and Tandem lands on any Linux.
  * The bundle carries the exact same files as the `.deb`, from build.py's one
    LAYOUT - so the two can never drift - plus a generated MANIFEST and two
    static scripts, packaging/install.sh and packaging/uninstall.sh. install.sh
    copies each file to its place with its own mode (honouring DESTDIR so it is
    testable and packageable), then does what the .deb's
    postinst does: refreshes the desktop, MIME and icon caches, runs the
    per-user first-run setup as the invoking user, and prints the install notice
    read from the catalogue in the machine's language. uninstall.sh removes
    exactly what the MANIFEST lists and prunes the empty directories.
  * The .deb build is byte-for-byte unchanged - the tarball is an addition, not
    a change - and the whole install/uninstall cycle is exercised in the suite
    against a DESTDIR, asserting every file lands with the right mode and that
    uninstall leaves nothing behind. packaging/*.sh join the bash -n and
    shellcheck gates. No new product message; no translation step. This is the
    universal foundation the PKGBUILD and .rpm rungs build on next.

