tandem (4.42) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Two false verdicts on a double-click, both found by the field-test and audit
    sweep, both of the class this project exists to abolish - the software
    saying something untrue about what just happened.
  * An AppImage whose download stopped BEFORE its payload even began is now
    recognized as truncated instead of run. The reader lumped "cannot work out
    the payload offset" together with "the file is shorter than that offset", so
    a download cut early read COMPLETO=? rather than 0; the handler's guard is
    `[ "$COMPLETO" = 0 ]`, so it fell through to chmod +x and handed a half-
    downloaded binary to the kernel, whose ENOEXEC surfaced bash's own "cannot
    execute binary file" - Tandem's script path and line number - to the owner
    as if the program had said it. appimageinfo.py now returns "truncated" for
    that case; it is one-sided (a complete AppImage always ends its payload at
    or before EOF), and a regression test proves the handler refuses and never
    makes the half-file executable.
  * A silent installer is no longer branded a flash-and-vanish failure. 4.40
    exempted installers that are a `.msi`/`.msp` or that laid a new Start Menu
    shortcut, but a runtime, a driver or a `setup.exe /S` registers neither and
    still exits in a second - so it was branded "opened and closed on its own",
    and in a window the owner clicks No, writing CONFIRMADO=nao and poisoning
    the memory, the recipe and the list for a program that installed fine. A
    third signal closes it: a new Add/Remove-Programs entry, read from the
    prefix's registry files with no Wine. The decision is now a pure
    `t_run_foi_instalador` (`.msi`/`.msp`, or a new shortcut, or a new uninstall
    entry) pinned in every direction - including that a real flash-and-vanish
    program, which adds none of the three, is still caught.
  * No new user-facing message; no translation step.

 -- Tandem <rockx0@gmail.com>  Thu, 20 Aug 2026 03:00:00 +0000

tandem (4.41) unstable; urgency=high

  * Security: a malicious .deb could run arbitrary code as the user when opened,
    before any password was asked. Its Installed-Size control field was placed
    inside a shell $(( )) to show "this will use N MB", and bash evaluates an
    arithmetic operand RECURSIVELY - so an Installed-Size of  a[$(command)]  ran
    that command. Two guards close it: debinfo.py emits Installed-Size only when
    it is all digits (Debian policy makes it an integer), and tandem-deb refuses
    to feed a non-numeric value to the arithmetic. A regression test builds a
    hostile .deb and proves that reading it executes nothing.

