tandem (4.37) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix two false "everything looks healthy" answers in `tandem saude` - the
    project's own cardinal sin (false reassurance instead of silence) committed
    by the very command built to catch problems. Both were found by a measured
    audit that RAN the command rather than reading it, and both reproduced.
  * Recovery readiness now checks the backup's STRUCTURE, not just its checksum.
    saude asked `t_backup_verifica`, which only compares the sidecar hash - so a
    0-byte or truncated archive with no `.sha256` beside it (one `tar` cannot
    even open) passed as a valid recovery net. It now asks `t_restauravel`, the
    same pre-flight a real restore runs, so a structurally broken backup is
    flagged "act now" instead of waved through. `t_saude_backup_veredito` takes
    that verdict token now and condemns danificado / nao-e-backup / corrompido.
  * A web service that is up but serving nothing now shows as a problem. The
    service loop passed the service NAME where `t_servico_escuta` /
    `t_servico_responde` expect a PORT, and captured their (empty) stdout - not
    their exit code - so the verdict never became `escuta-mudo` and that branch
    was dead code. It now reads the port with `t_servico_le NAME PORTA` and maps
    the exit codes to sim / nao, so a service that listens but answers nothing
    is caught; a check it cannot run (no curl) stays silent, not a false alarm.
  * No new message keys; one existing one reworded to be accurate and less
    jargon - the backup warning said "fails its checksum", which is wrong for a
    0-byte archive that has no checksum, so it now says "is damaged" (re-read in
    all seven languages). New proof-by-injection tests: the backup verdict on a
    broken-structure archive, an up-but-mute service reaching `escuta-mudo`, and
    both end to end through `tandem saude`.

