tandem (4.36) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix a hang in `tandem doctor` and `tandem socorro` at an interactive
    terminal - worse than the silence this project exists to abolish, and
    invisible to CI. The 4.26 web-service feature added a SECOND function named
    `t_porta_escutando` (a stdin parser fed by a pipe), and bash keeps the last
    definition of a name. So the older `t_porta_escutando` - the one that runs
    `ss` itself, which the dongle check `t_chave_estado` calls with no pipe -
    was shadowed, and the call read the terminal's stdin and blocked forever.
    `tandem doctor` calls it for the Sentinel and CodeMeter keys on every run,
    and `tandem socorro` runs doctor, so both hung with zero output at a
    terminal.
    CI never saw it because the suite feeds a non-terminal stdin, where the
    parser hits EOF and returns at once - which also silently broke the dongle
    port check, since that EOF path always answered "not listening".
  * The runner is renamed `t_porta_ouvindo_ss` (its one caller repointed); the
    stdin parser keeps the name and its pipe callers. And because the real
    defect was "two features silently share a name", the fix ships the
    INSTRUMENT for the class, not just the instance: a test that fails if any
    function in common.sh is defined twice, proven by putting the collision
    back. A second test pins that `t_chave_estado` never reads stdin.
  * Two adjacent guards found in the same adversarial sweep, each behind its own
    test. The `tandem saude` Wine-changed check (4.35) trimmed: a memory file
    carrying a CR or an unterminated last line could fire a false "Wine changed"
    even when the versions match - the value is now normalised before the
    compare, so it cannot cry wolf on a file edited or moved between machines.
    And the "dialout" group warning in `tandem portas` is now gated on a serial
    device actually existing, mirroring its "lp" sibling, so the report can no
    longer say "no serial port on this computer" and warn about one in the same
    breath.

