tandem (4.34) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Round two: pin the Wine that works, so an update cannot swap it out from
    under a working POS. 4.28 named the cause when a program that opened cleanly
    yesterday breaks today because a distro upgrade changed Wine underneath it;
    4.34 offers the way to stop it happening again.
  * When Tandem sees that a program opened before under one Wine and the system
    now carries a different one, and Wine is not already pinned, it adds one
    honest line to that message: you CAN pin Wine at a version so the next
    update cannot move it - `sudo apt-mark hold wine` - but that also holds back
    Wine security updates, so it is your call, and `sudo apt-mark unhold wine`
    undoes it. It EXPLAINS and never executes: Tandem runs no apt-mark itself,
    the same rule that keeps it from installing a .deb it detected.
  * It never nags without cause. The offer appears only where there is a real
    reason - a program that worked under a specific Wine - never as a blanket
    suggestion to hold a package, because pinning has a real cost. The decision
    is a pure function (`t_wine_pin_veredito`: pode-fixar / ja-fixado /
    sem-referencia / sem-wine) with a proof-by-injection test; reading whether
    Wine is held is machine-only (`apt-mark showhold`), like the Wine read.
  * Tooling: adds `tools/preenche-traducao.py`, the reusable catalogue-fill
    instrument, so the multi-line translation step stops being re-derived by
    hand each version - built with a self-test and used for this version's
    message.

