tandem (4.30) unstable; urgency=medium

  * First of round two, the deeper round: verifiable backups. A counter's disk
    dies and the shop needs last night's backup to come back on a replacement
    PC - and a backup nobody can prove is intact is the worst silent failure of
    all, the kind found only at the moment it is needed.
  * `tandem backup` now writes a checksum beside the archive - the same .sha256
    sidecar, and the same discipline, this project uses to prove its own
    releases three ways. `tandem backup verificar <file>` (alias `verify`) reads
    that checksum back and says plainly whether the backup is intact, corrupted,
    or has no checksum to check against - so the owner can test a backup the day
    he makes it, not the day he needs it.
  * A restore no longer trusts that the archive merely OPENS. Before it wipes
    the working environment to put the backup in place, it verifies the archive
    against its checksum, and refuses a corrupted one with a clear sentence
    rather than deleting a good setup to lay down a broken one. When there is no
    checksum beside the backup - an older one, or a hand-copied file - it says
    so honestly and falls back to the structural check it always did.
  * The integrity decision is a pure function (`t_backup_verifica`), tested by
    corrupting a byte and watching it refuse; the checksum read is a second one.
    Tandem still writes only where the owner points and never into a Wine prefix
    it did not create.

