tandem (4.27) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The first of an extrapolation round: Tandem now catches the silent failures
    that happen AROUND the software, not only inside it. `tandem relogio` (alias
    `clock`) checks the machine's clock BEFORE a program fails because of it.
  * A wrong clock breaks TLS, software licensing and Brazilian fiscal software
    (NF-e/NFC-e) in silence - and the usual cause is a dead CMOS battery that
    resets the date on every boot. Tandem already inferred this REACTIVELY, from
    the certificate error a program throws after it fails; this makes it a
    first-class, proactive check that runs before the failure.
  * The verdict never cries wolf on a correct clock. The one bulletproof signal
    is that the clock cannot predate the software running on it: if "now" is
    before this package's own release date (read from its changelog), the clock
    is certainly behind - the exact shape a battery reset makes. An absurd far
    future is flagged too; a plausible date with automatic time turned off is a
    gentle "turn it on so it stays right", not an alarm.
  * Reading the clock needs no privilege; SETTING it does - so, like the
    printer-port remedy, Tandem PRINTS the exact command
    (`sudo timedatectl set-ntp true`, or `set-time`) rather than running sudo in
    silence. `tandem doctor` gained a one-line clock status for the same reason.
  * The logic is pure and tested with proof by injection - a date before the
    release, an absurd future, and a correct date each get their verdict, and
    reverting the guard turns the test red. The live `timedatectl` read is
    machine-only, exactly like the Wine and systemd checks. New messages are
    translated in all seven languages.

