tandem (4.26) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A TENTH thing Tandem carries, and the first that is not a file you double
    click: a web service. `tandem servico` takes a folder that is meant to run
    and stay running - a Node, Python, PHP, Java, a plain binary, a shell
    script, or a Windows server .exe under Wine - and does the part a shopkeeper
    should never have to learn: it detects what the folder is, writes a
    systemd --user unit for it, starts it, tries to make it survive a reboot,
    and tells you the address to open. No root, no /etc, no jargon.
  * `tandem servico` on its own answers the only question that matters, per
    service, in plain language: is it running, is something listening on its
    port, and does it actually answer. `abrir` opens it in the browser;
    `iniciar`, `parar`, `reiniciar` and `remover` are the rest; `registros`
    shows what it printed, without a journalctl incantation.
  * The diagnosis is the whole point, and every failure path says why instead
    of nothing: a service that crashed on start shows its last lines; a port
    already taken names who is holding it (ss, then lsof); a service that is up
    but not answering yet is told apart from one that is truly broken; and a
    machine with no user systemd session is told so plainly rather than failing
    mute.
  * Reboot survival needs `loginctl enable-linger`, which is the one privileged
    step - so it is best-effort and, when it needs a password, the exact one
    command to run is printed rather than the service silently dying at the next
    boot.
  * Carries the 4.25 fix, which never shipped on its own: `tandem portas fixar`
    wrote only the registry key and not the dosdevices/comN symlink, so the port
    it "fixed" still could not be opened - the printer/pinpad remedy this file
    documents, reporting success and opening nothing. Both halves are written
    now, and soltar removes both.

