tandem (4.15) unstable; urgency=medium

  * "1 minuto(s)" is what a program says when it cannot count, and nine
    messages said it. The parentheses were never laziness: the catalogue could
    not carry a second form, so the only honest thing a translator could write
    was the dodge. Plural forms were one of the two reasons this project moved
    to gettext in the first place, and they had never worked.
  * The measured defect underneath is worse than the wording. tools/po-para-
    catalogo.py DROPPED any .po entry carrying msgid_plural - "msgstr[0] " does
    not start with "msgstr ", so the whole entry fell through the parser. No
    output, no warning, exit 0. Proven by appending a probe entry to po/en.po
    and watching the compiled catalogue come back without it, reporting the
    same count as before. The people that silently discards are exactly the
    volunteers the format was adopted to attract, and getting five unreviewed
    catalogues read by native speakers is the top item on this project's list.
  * THE RULE IS CODE AND THE FORMS ARE DATA. gettext ships its plural rule as a
    C expression inside the file; honouring one would mean $(( )) around text
    that arrived from a stranger, undoing the property this format exists to
    have - that a message can never be code, which has its own test.
    So t_plural_indice is shell, and the .po carries the rule as documentation
    for Poedit and Weblate. Two tables, and a test that pins them together.
  * Nine messages, seven languages, four different rules: English and Spanish
    put zero with the plural, Portuguese and French put it with the singular
    ("0 minuto"), Chinese has one form, and Arabic has six. Exercised in all
    six at 0, 1, 2, 3, 11 and 100.
  * A form numbered past the end of a language's own rule is now REFUSED, by
    name, with a non-zero exit - it can never be reached by any count, so
    shipping it quietly would spend somebody's afternoon and deliver nothing.
  * The seeding branch written to protect the migration was removed rather than
    kept: the fuzzy mark drops the entry before it can run, so the code read as
    a safety net while being unreachable. Chinese, Hindi and Arabic instead
    carry the sentence they were already shipping, in every form their language
    has - not a new translation, and it should not be described as one.
  * "took (s):" on the memory screen was never a plural at all - the (s) was an
    abbreviation for seconds, and it reads as one of these dodges. It is
    "seconds:" now, aligned with the labels above it. The three languages whose
    label uses a native abbreviation keep it.

