tandem (4.13) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Tandem says when there is a newer Tandem, and never installs it. The owner
    left the choice to me and this is the half that needs nothing from him: it
    reads the version number of the newest published release once a day, in the
    background, and says so once. The fetch never delays a command - the answer
    is used on the NEXT run, exactly like the community list.
  * IT WILL NOT UPDATE ITSELF, and that is a decision rather than an unfinished
    edge. Fetching the list is data that only ever becomes a suggestion; a .deb
    is code that runs as root. The moment Tandem can replace its own binary from
    the internet, whoever controls the release address owns every shop machine
    at once - and the machine this was born on runs a point-of-sale system.
    That is rule No 1 turned on Tandem itself. A test asserts the whole path
    contains no apt-get, no dpkg -i, no t_como_root, no pkexec and no sudo.
  * The proper destination is an apt repository, where the update arrives
    through the updater the owner already trusts and already gets asked about.
    That needs a signing key, which is a secret only he can make, so it waits
    for him - this exists in the meantime rather than instead of it.
  * dpkg does the comparing, because a string compare calls 4.9 newer than
    4.10 - which is this project's own numbering. sort -V is the fallback and
    "equal" answers no in both.
  * The off switch turns off the thing he can SEE. Gating only the fetch left
    the version a previous run had already stored, so "tandem versao
    nao-avisar" stopped the network call and went on printing the notice for
    ever. Found by running the four states in order, not by reading the code.


  * The list finally opens the field that says what did NOT work. Field 4 of
    the record - the components installed that did not fix it - has been
    written by every client, accepted by the intake, published by the rebuild
    and downloaded to every machine since 3.4, and read by NOBODY:
    t_lista_linha's awk touches fields 1, 3, 5, 6 and 7. So a shop could be one
    click away from half an hour of dotnet48 that sixty other shops had already
    burned on this exact installer, with that fact sitting on its own disk.
  * The sentence is attached to the QUESTION, which is the only moment it is
    worth anything: after the install starts, knowing is too late. It weighs
    the reports the same way the resolver does, so an old report from a
    different Wine counts for less here too, and it sums across rows - the
    thing the read path got wrong once already.
  * It warns and never vetoes. A component that failed on somebody else's
    machine can be exactly right on this one, so it adds a sentence and changes
    no default; the owner is the one deciding whether to spend the afternoon.
    A test asserts it never removes anything from the offer.
  * And "nobody got this working" is finally readable: a row whose verbs field
    is "-" is a machine saying it tried and nothing helped - the record the
    intake goes out of its way to accept and the resolver drops on its first
    line. It answers only when there is no working lesson at all, because one
    shop failing where four hundred succeeded is a fact about that shop.

  * THE LOG IS CUT BY A MARKER, NOT BY COUNTING LINES - and this one was caught
    by CI disagreeing with itself. Two runs of an IDENTICAL commit, one green
    and one red, on a test whose second pass suddenly failed to detect a DLL
    that was plainly in the log. tandem-exe took MARCA=$(wc -l < "$LOG") and
    later sliced with tail -n +$((MARCA+1)), which is only correct while this
    process is the ONLY writer - and Tandem now spawns background work into the
    same file, the community-list fetch and the version check. A writer landing
    between the count and the slice moves the window, and the detector reads
    the wrong part of the log.
  * The owner saw the same shape from the other side on his own machine the
    same day: lines from "tandem socorro" appearing under the heading of
    "tandem version". One cause, two symptoms, and the flaky CI run is what
    made it undeniable.
  * A marker is written INTO the stream it will be used to cut, so whatever
    else lands before or after it, the text after the marker is exactly the
    text written after the marker. Distinct per process and per attempt, and
    the marker itself never comes back in the slice - it would otherwise be
    shown to the owner as if the program had said it, which is the defect 4.5
    fixed for tandem-script.

  * A lesson taken from the list can finally be corroborated back into it.
    INSTALADOS_AGORA was only ever appended to inside the detector loop, so a
    lesson APPLIED from the list or from a recipe left RESOLVERAM empty, the
    record was never built, and the count on that lesson could never rise above
    the shops that had rediscovered it from scratch - which is precisely what
    the shortcut exists to stop happening.
  * Field 12 keeps the two apart: "proprio" is a shop that worked it out for
    itself, "aplicado" is one that applied somebody else's and found it worked.
    They must NEVER be summed, or four hundred machines applying one suggestion
    read as eight hundred discovering it - the list confirming itself out of
    its own output. So "aplicado" rows never select a lesson and are counted
    separately, and a row from before 4.13 reads as "proprio", which is what it
    was.
  * The first attempt at this put the initialisation of INSTALADOS_AGORA BELOW
    the shortcut that fills it, silently wiping it - caught by reading the
    order, since no test reaches that path.
 -- Tandem <rockx0@gmail.com>  Sat, 15 Aug 2026 18:40:00 +0000

tandem (4.12) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The panel shows which Tandem this is, and stops showing its own plumbing.
    It is the only screen a shop owner who never opens a terminal ever sees,
    and to find out what version he was running he had to open a terminal -
    the exact thing this program exists to spare him. The version is in the
    title now. The owner asked for this in one sentence and was right in two:
    the second half of his question was why the program meant to simplify
    things is itself awkward.
  * The awkward half was the first COLUMN. Every row led with the internal
    command name - "identidade", "restore", "autoteste" - words he has to
    decode before he can read the sentence beside them, and decoding them buys
    him nothing, because he is clicking a row and not typing a command. zenity
    can return a column without displaying it (--hide-column with
    --print-column), so the token still reaches `case "$esc" in` untouched and
    the panel became one column of plain sentences. Verified with xdotool
    driving a real zenity under Xvfb: with column 1 hidden it still answers
    "instalar".

  * The memory screen was printing on-disk format at the owner. Found in the
    field, on his machine, on a real .deb built for arm64 - the LIMITE line
    reached him as:
      limite: arquitetura|Este pacote foi feito para... .\n\nEle e para arm64
    Two leaks in one line. "arquitetura|" is the internal separator carrying
    the CLASS of the limit, and the \n were literal backslash-n: t_memoria_grava
    escapes newlines so a value stays on one line and t_memoria_le undoes it
    with printf '%b' on read - which the sed field map in acao_memoria bypassed
    entirely. LIMITE is the only field there that is neither a single word nor
    a plain sentence, and it was the one that walked around the rule.

  * The self-test stopped setting off a red alarm to prove alarms work. Its
    first check calls t_erro for real, and t_erro on a graphical machine fires
    a CRITICAL desktop notification - so running the self-test popped
    "self-test: ignore this message" at the owner, and `tandem socorro`, which
    embeds the self-test, popped it at somebody already in trouble and reading
    carefully. Found in a log from his machine, not here.
  * The shape of the mistake is worth more than the fix. It was written
    `[ -n "$(t_erro ...)" ] || t_tem_gui`, and bash evaluates the left side
    FIRST - so the alarm fired on exactly the machines where the `|| t_tem_gui`
    then made the result independent of it. It frightened the owner to learn
    nothing. Running the probe with the display unset exercises MORE than
    before, not less: the message-building path is now checked on every machine
    instead of being short-circuited away on the graphical ones.


  * "tandem restore" tells you how to make the thing it is missing. It said
    "No backup found in your home folder." and stopped there - naming the
    problem and not the way out, which is the one thing this project treats as
    a defect rather than a limitation. The owner hit it from the panel and
    reasonably went looking for a .tar.gz to DOWNLOAD; there is none, because
    Tandem is what makes it. The message now names "tandem backup", and draws
    the distinction that matters on a shop machine: backup saves the
    ENVIRONMENT, which Tandem can rebuild anyway, while "tandem dados" saves
    HIS files, which nothing can.

  * ...and "tandem backup" says how to put it back. It told the owner about
    "tandem dados salvar" and never once named "tandem restore", so the only
    affordance left to him was to double-click the .tar.gz - which unpacks a
    Wine prefix into a pile of files in his Downloads folder and restores
    nothing. The mirror of the defect above, on the other side of the same
    pair, found the same way: by him using it.
  * Tandem does NOT claim .tar.gz, and will not. Claiming application/gzip
    would take every archive on the machine away from the archive manager,
    which is the exact harm the association work exists to avoid - so the fix
    is a sentence telling him not to click it, not an association.

  * A COMMAND TYPED AT A PROMPT ANSWERS AT THAT PROMPT. The owner typed
    "tandem backup" and the terminal returned nothing at all - "simplesmente
    nao retornou nada". The command had a perfectly good answer for him, and
    t_erro handed it to notify-send because a graphical session existed. It
    went into a bubble over his desktop while he sat looking at a silent
    prompt.
  * This is the cardinal rule of the whole project broken from the inside, and
    not in one command: t_erro, t_ok and t_aviso all preferred the desktop
    whenever a desktop existed, so EVERY command typed at a terminal on a
    machine that has one - which is every shop machine there is - could answer
    into thin air. t_texto has had the right rule since 3.4 and the other three
    never adopted it.
  * The rule, now shared as t_tem_terminal: a notification is what you use when
    there is NOBODY at a terminal, which is the double-click case. When
    somebody is there, that is where they are looking. Both descriptors are
    checked, because a caller may redirect either one and still have a person
    at the other. For t_erro the terminal wins OUTRIGHT rather than as well - a
    modal dialog that blocks until somebody clicks it is the wrong answer to a
    typed command.
  * Measured both ways under a real tty with a display present: the old code
    produced ZERO BYTES, and the double-click path still notifies.
  * "tandem backup" also explains which environment it means. It said "There is
    no Windows environment to save yet." to somebody with TWO Wine prefixes on
    the machine, which reads as a lie. It now says Tandem has not created one
    YET, that opening a Windows program through it creates one, and that the
    profiles he already had are his - never written into, and for the same
    reason never backed up.
