tandem (4.11) unstable; urgency=medium

  * "tandem repair" asks who owns a type with the tool the file manager
    actually uses. This repository proved two versions ago that xdg-mime is the
    wrong instrument - Nautilus uses GIO, and GIO resolves the MIME SUBCLASS
    CHAIN while xdg-mime does not - and the command had it exactly half right:
    it WRITES the association through both tools and read it back through
    xdg-mime alone. So the before/after report, which is the entire point of
    that command and the thing the owner reads to find out who held the type,
    could answer "nobody" about a type a text editor really owns.
  * .flatpakref is the case that makes it matter: it is declared sub-class-of
    text/plain, so with Tandem's association gone a double-clicked one opens in
    a TEXT EDITOR rather than doing nothing - and "nobody" is then both wrong
    and a milder story than the truth. Re-measured on this machine: gio mime
    text/sgml answers vim.desktop and xdg-mime query default answers nothing.
  * xdg-mime stays as the fallback rather than as the authority, because a
    machine with no GIO is one where it is the only answer available - not one
    where its answer is as good. Setting the association still goes through
    both tools, since setting it in one is not enough.

  * A third instrument: tools/chaves-e-chamadas.py checks the catalogue against
    the code in BOTH directions and checks placeholder parity. The third of
    those is the one that puts nonsense on a counter - a message reading "it
    asks for Java {1} and the one here is {2}" called with one argument renders
    a literal "{2}" to the owner, and nothing in this tree could see that.
  * Its own first version produced NINE findings and all nine were wrong: it
    counted the words inside a "$(...)" argument and it matched a t_msg named
    inside a COMMENT. That is the failure this project fears most in an
    instrument - a count that is mostly noise gets ignored, and that is how a
    real finding goes unread. It refuses to guess now: a call whose arguments
    nest a substitution is reported as UNCHECKED, never as clean. 88% of the
    732 call sites are checked and the remaining 86 are named as unchecked.
  * Proven by injecting the defect it exists for, with an assert on the
    injection - because the first attempt at that proof silently replaced
    nothing and "passed".

  * The delivery proof finally reaches the lesson. Since 3.3 Tandem has checked
    that the DLL a program asked for really arrived, and since 3.4 that it
    arrived in a width the program can load - three distinct outcomes, computed
    on every install and then thrown away. The memory recorded only whether the
    OWNER answered. So "I verified the missing file arrived and he closed the
    window without clicking" and "there was nothing I could check and he closed
    the window without clicking" both came out "so-abriu", and both travelled to
    somebody else's machine, in a recipe and in a list record, as the same word.
  * The memory files gain PROVA=, with four levels: nao-chegou (provably still
    missing), bitola-errada (arrived in a width this program cannot load),
    entregue (proven to arrive, correctly) and sem-alvo (there was no file to
    check for - half the winetricks verbs have no same-named DLL). That last
    one is the distinction the whole field exists for: "I could not check" is
    not "nothing was wrong".
  * Only "entregue" becomes a confidence level, and the community list weighs it
    at HALF a report. It is real evidence about the FILE and the question the
    list answers is about the PROGRAM - a DLL landing in system32 does not mean
    a shop's accounting package opens. Sixty people who looked at a screen
    outweigh a hundred proven deliveries; two hundred proven deliveries outweigh
    sixty people. Both directions are pinned by tests so the constant cannot be
    quietly rounded up to one.
  * A lesson with a proven delivery and nobody to ask is now offered to the
    list, where before it died on the machine: with no answer there was no
    confidence, and with no confidence there was nothing worth passing on. It
    goes out labelled "entregue", which states plainly that nobody confirmed it.
  * The ordering rule became a function in the library rather than four lines
    inside the install loop, and the reason is that the first version of it was
    a plain assignment - which let the LAST DLL of the LAST verb speak for the
    whole run, so a wrong-width found after a missing file reported the milder
    of the two. That is the same shape as the MARCA_WT defect 4.8 fixed one
    screen higher up. Inline, the only way to reach it is to make a real
    winetricks fail; extracted, every pair that could be got backwards is a
    test.
  * The counter caught the three on-disk tokens this added to tandem-exe, which
    is what it is for - they went on the exceptions list with the reason, beside
    "confirmado" and "abriu", rather than into the catalogues.

  * "tandem memoria" shows the owner's own answer, which it had never once
    displayed: `grep CONFIRMADO src/bin/tandem` returned nothing, while the
    screen faithfully printed RESULTADO=abriu - a value the exit code sets and
    the "no" branch deliberately leaves alone. So the one program he had told
    Tandem was broken came back to him as "result: it opened", and "tandem
    socorro" embeds this screen verbatim, meaning the report he sends to
    whoever is helping him ASSERTED that the program works.
  * His answer is printed FIRST, above the exit code's verdict, and there is a
    test for the order: a screen that leads with "it opened" and mentions the
    rejection further down is read as "it worked" by anybody skimming. The
    RESULTADO line stays underneath rather than being overwritten - "it
    launched and he says it does not work" is a more useful pair of facts than
    either one alone, and it is the shape of the case somebody helping him
    needs to see.
  * Pinned by running the command rather than the library. This whole class of
    defect lives in the gap between what a function stores and what a screen
    prints, and no test of t_confirma_funcionou could ever have seen it.

  * A snap and a flatpak are found in the menu. t_atalhos_do_sistema looked in
    /usr/share/applications and /usr/local/share/applications and nowhere else,
    while snapd exports to /var/lib/snapd/desktop/applications and flatpak to
    its own directories under /var/lib/flatpak and ~/.local/share/flatpak. So
    "tandem programas"' whole promise - GNOME under Wayland does not re-read the
    menu, and a program the owner cannot find again is a program he has not
    installed - was quietly unkept for three of the four package managers since
    3.8, and tandem-snap's "look in the menu for" line had never once appeared
    on anybody's screen. tandem-flatpak did not call it at all.
  * XDG_DATA_DIRS and XDG_DATA_HOME are asked first, because they are the right
    answer and they pick up whatever else a distribution invents - and then the
    three directories are named explicitly anyway, because the variables are not
    enough. That is measured, not assumed: in this project's own container BOTH
    variables are EMPTY while all three export directories exist and hold files,
    which is exactly the shape of a program started from a file manager rather
    than from a login shell. The list is deduplicated, since on a real desktop
    those paths overlap and a doubled entry would announce every program twice.

  * No catalogue message is handed to printf as its FORMAT STRING any more.
    Four were - prep_terminal, desinst_terminal and the two flatpak prompts -
    and they carried printf's %s in all seven catalogues, which is the one
    format this project decided against. The value being safe was never the
    point: these files will one day arrive from strangers, and a translator who
    writes "100% seguro" breaks a prompt in a language nobody here reads.
    Measured rather than asserted - under the old form that sentence rendered
    as "100eguro", because printf ate the "% s". A translator is not somebody
    who should have to know what a printf conversion is.
  * "tandem preparar" and "tandem desinstalar" accept the letter their own
    screen asks for. Both still ran `case "$r" in s|S|sim|SIM)` after printing
    [y/N] in English and [o/N] in French - the exact correctness defect
    t_confirmou was written for, fixed in all five HANDLERS and missed here
    twice because the CLI is not a handler. Same scoping shape as the
    thirteenth miss of the literal counter. These are the two commands where
    the alternative to acting is being told nothing happened.
  * Two guards, both proven by putting the defect back: no catalogue may carry
    a printf conversion, and no message may be used as a format string.

  * "tandem socorro" gets the report to a second human. Its last step was
    t_ok, and t_ok RETURNS as soon as notify-send succeeds - so on a graphical
    machine the whole thing was a ten-second toast, and the owner was left to
    find a file in his home directory from a notification that had vanished.
    This is the "send me the diagnosis" button.
  * What went with it matters more than the path: the third paragraph is the
    one that makes the feature defensible - the file shows names and paths of
    HIS files, and a path sometimes carries a customer's name. He only really
    decides to send it if he has read that, and a toast is not somewhere
    anybody reads. It goes in a window now, with the path on the clipboard and
    a checked xdg-open on the folder - the same two shortcuts "tandem
    contribuir" has had all along, thirty lines above it in the same file.
  * The clipboard half became t_copia_para_area in the library. Inline in
    acao_contribuir it could not be shared, which is exactly why socorro spent
    two versions ending in a toast while its neighbour was already doing this
    properly.
  * The defect is GUI-ONLY - on a terminal t_ok prints the whole text and the
    old code looked fine - so the guard is structural: a test that cannot open
    a window checks that a window is what gets asked for.

  * The community list is received without anybody typing a command.
    t_lista_atualiza had exactly ONE caller in the whole tree - "tandem lista
    atualizar", typed by hand - so on a machine whose owner has never heard of
    that command the list file never existed, the query always answered
    nothing, and every merge rule 4.4, 4.9 and 4.11 added was unreachable code.
    Meanwhile SENDING has been on by default since 4.2. A machine that gives
    and does not take is the wrong way round.
  * This reverses "Automatic sync on install - REJECTED" in docs/IDEAS.md, and
    the argument is narrow enough to state in one line: 4.2 already reversed
    that stance for the direction that carries data OUT, and this direction
    carries nothing out. It only ever DOWNLOADS.
  * It mirrors the send path deliberately: a named off-switch ("tandem lista
    desligar"), whose state is on the "tandem lista" screen either way - an
    automatic thing the owner cannot see is an automatic thing he cannot turn
    off - and at most one fetch a day. The stamp is written BEFORE the attempt,
    not after, so a machine with no route to the address makes ONE failed
    request a day instead of one per double click. That is the same lesson the
    send path learned when a cap counting only successes turned out not to be
    a cap at all.
  * DETACHED, and the cost is stated rather than hidden: the fetch never delays
    a double click, so a list fetched now helps from the NEXT run and not this
    one. That is a real limitation, and it beats helping on no run ever.

  * The machine writes down what it has sent. Of the three branches in the send
    path, the sieve refusal and the 4xx refusal both PARK their line under an
    explicit written rule - and the one branch that succeeded was the only one
    that destroyed it. So "what has this machine sent about my shop?" had no
    answer on the machine, and that is the question a shopkeeper, or whoever
    audits him, actually asks.
  * This is not tidiness. Section 3 of docs/IDEAS.md rejected telemetry on the
    rule "nothing the owner cannot see and cannot delete", and what had left
    this machine was neither. So both halves are built: "tandem enviar" shows
    the running total and where to read the whole thing, and "tandem esquecer
    enviados" erases it - saying plainly that erasing changes nothing at the
    far end, because what is sent is sent.
  * The MONTH is recorded, not the day, for the same reason the published
    record carries only the month: a date with a day identifies. And
    "tandem socorro" sweeps only *.log, so the ledger is never swept into a
    report the owner sends to somebody else.

  * An unreviewed translation says so to the people who did not choose it.
    t_idioma_revisado had exactly two call sites, both inside "tandem idioma" -
    the command a person types to CHANGE the language. But the language is
    normally resolved from the system locale with nobody typing anything, which
    is how almost everybody outside Brazil arrives. So this project's own
    principle - shipping an unreviewed translation is defensible, shipping it
    without saying so is not - was honoured only for the minority, and everybody
    else got the unreviewed prose with the honesty stripped off.
  * It is also the only recruitment channel the five unreviewed catalogues
    have: the people running them are native speakers who do not know there is
    anything to review. The notice says how to help and is three lines, not the
    seven-line paragraph shown to somebody who asked.
  * On stderr, never a dialog, once per (language, version) - a new version
    adds new lines to those catalogues, so it is worth repeating then and not
    before, and a notice on every command is one people learn to scroll past.
    It names the LANGUAGE, not the locale code. And it is deliberately NOT
    wired into tandem-exe, with a test asserting so: nothing belongs between
    the double click and the program.
