tandem (4.6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The community list has an address. It was empty for five versions because
    an address means somebody hosts it and answers for the data, which is a
    decision with a cost rather than a line of code. The receiving end is in
    THIS repository - api/lista.js - deployed to a Vercel project git-linked to
    it, so the code that takes a shop's data is as readable as the code that
    sends it. A service in its own repository is how "we only collect anonymous
    data" becomes a claim nobody can check.
  * No secret is created by hand anywhere in the chain. Vercel injects the
    storage credential when the store is connected, and the job that rebuilds
    the list reads a public URL. Every other design ended with somebody pasting
    a token somewhere, and avoiding that was the requirement.
  * The intake does not trust the sender. The client sieves a line before
    queueing it, but anybody can craft a POST by hand, so every rule is applied
    again server-side. It keeps no address, in any form. And it refuses a record
    that claims to speak for more than one machine, because a number anybody can
    type is a number that means nothing.
  * Nothing publishes itself: a weekly job rebuilds lista/lista.tsv and opens a
    PULL REQUEST. A poisoned row that published itself would be downloaded by
    every Tandem at once, while the same row reaching one machine is a question
    its owner gets asked. Those are different sizes of mistake.
  * The install notice has promised since 4.2 that Tandem sends what it learns,
    and with no address configured nothing ever left. Setting it does not only
    enable a feature - it makes a sentence already on the screen true.
  * A 4xx used to be treated as a failure of the route. It is not: the far end
    saying THIS LINE is wrong will say it for ever, so the line was retried on
    every pass, and three permanently refused lines would trip the hour-long
    wait meant for a broken network - stopping the good lines from leaving. One
    malformed record would have poisoned the whole queue. A 4xx now parks the
    line rather than deleting it; 429 and 408 stay retryable, because too fast
    and too slow are about the moment and not about the line.
  * The count is of REPORTS, and the sentence the owner reads said "machines" in
    all seven languages. Counting machines honestly needs a way to tell two
    senders apart, which is exactly what the intake refuses to keep - and "we
    only store a hash" does not survive contact with a laptop, because a hash of
    an IPv4 address is an IPv4 address to anybody willing to try four billion of
    them. The claim shrank rather than the number growing.
  * tandem-apk was the only one of the nine handlers that remembered nothing -
    not even a RESULTADO - so "tandem memoria" knew nothing about an .apk and a
    second attempt learned nothing from the first. The guard written for it
    distinguishes a verdict about the FILE, which is a lesson, from a verdict
    about THIS MACHINE, which is not: recording "adb is missing" would mean
    installing adb and still reading a memory that blames the file.

 -- ChrnX0 <rockx0@gmail.com>  Thu, 13 Aug 2026 17:53:53 +0000

tandem (4.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A shell installer that exits 0 was the one place left where "it finished"
    still meant "it worked". There is no database to ask afterwards, so the exit
    code was the whole story - but there is other evidence, the same the silent-
    success guard uses for a program: an installer that finishes almost at once
    having printed nothing at all has not installed anything. Tandem cannot
    prove that either, so it says what it saw instead of congratulating anybody.
  * And writing that guard found a defect it could never have fired past. The
    success path read the WHOLE log to show "this is what it said", so what the
    owner saw under that heading was Tandem's own internal lines - in Portuguese
    - and a log with those lines in it is never empty. The failure path a few
    lines below had always sliced the log correctly; the success path never did.
    Found by running a script that does nothing and reading the output.
  * tandem-flatpak asked the authority backwards. It read "exit 0 OR flatpak
    agrees the program is there", so a 0 was proof on its own and flatpak was
    consulted only when the code had already said failure - the opposite of what
    tandem-deb spells out in three lines of comment for dpkg. Stated honestly:
    no case was found where flatpak install exits 0 without installing (a failed
    install exits 1, measured against flatpak 1.14.6). What changes is which
    question decides, and an error message followed by exit 0 would tell the
    desktop the double click had succeeded.

